import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const growthCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'p*(1 + r)^x', label: 'compounded', color: '#064e3b', width: 2.4 },
    { expr: 'p*(1 + r*x)', label: 'simple interest', color: 'rgba(23,36,32,0.5)' },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 'p', label: 'principal p', min: 1000, max: 100000, step: 500, value: 10000 },
    { name: 'r', label: 'annual rate r', min: 0, max: 0.15, step: 0.005, value: 0.08, unit: 'as decimal' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 40, label: 'years' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 360,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/finance/foundational/compound-interest">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Finance is the craft of moving value through time, and its first law is that <strong>money today
    is worth more than money later</strong> — because money today can be put to work and earn money.
    £100 in hand can become £108 in a year at 8%, so a promise of £100 next year is worth something
    less than £100 now. Every loan, bond, mortgage, pension and company valuation is an application of
    that one sentence. <strong>Compound interest</strong> is what happens when the earnings themselves
    start earning: growth on growth, year after year. It is the most important mechanism in personal
    finance, and it cuts both ways — it compounds debts exactly as enthusiastically as savings. This
    lesson builds the machinery forward (compounding), backward (discounting), and then into the
    harder truth: the rate r is not known in advance, so the second half of finance is quantitative
    tools — descriptive statistics, probability and risk measures — for handling a future that is a
    distribution, not a number.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the same rate, two maths — the gap between the curves is compounding, and it widens forever" widget="plotter" config={growthCfg} />

  <p>
    Slide the rate r: at 8%, compounded money roughly <strong>doubles every 9 years</strong> — the
    curve steepens noticeably by year 30. Simple interest stays a straight line forever: it pays the
    same pounds every year on the original principal, because the interest is (implicitly) spent, not
    reinvested. Compounding reinvests it, so each year's base is bigger than the last, and the straight
    line bends into an exponential. The gap between the two curves is not a rounding difference: over
    40 years at 8%, £10,000 grows to about £217,000 compounded but only £42,000 with simple interest —
    the reinvestment is worth five times the original deposit.
  </p>

  <h2>The arithmetic: one formula, forward and backward</h2>
  <p>
    The forward formula is FV = P × (1 + r)ⁿ: principal times the growth factor raised to the number of
    periods. Work it concretely: £10,000 at 8% for 3 years is 10,000 × 1.08³ = 10,000 × 1.2597 ≈
    £12,597 — year by year, £10,800, then £11,664, then £12,597.17, the last figure carrying the tell-tale
    extra pence of growth-on-growth. Compounding frequency matters more than intuition suggests: a
    nominal 12% paid monthly is 1% per month, which compounds to (1.01)¹² ≈ 12.68% per year — banks and
    card issuers quoting "APR" versus "effective annual rate" are separating exactly this effect, and
    the difference is why a mortgage's small print is worth reading.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>rule of 72</strong> is the working engineer's shortcut: divide 72 by the annual
    percentage rate to get the doubling time. 72 ÷ 8 = 9 years; 72 ÷ 12 = 6 years; 72 ÷ 3 = 24 years
    (which is why "risk-free" savings at 3% barely outpace inflation's own compounding — more on that
    below). The same rule runs the other way: a credit card balance at 36% APR doubles what you owe in
    two years, and the "minimum payment" trap is exponential arithmetic pointed at you — paying only
    the minimum on a £3,000 card balance at typical rates can take decades and cost multiples of the
    original debt, because most of each minimum payment is new interest.
  </p>
  <p>
    Run the formula backward and you get <strong>present value</strong> — the single most-used number
    in professional finance: PV = FV ÷ (1 + r)ⁿ. What is a promise of £10,000 in 10 years worth today
    at 8%? 10,000 ÷ 1.08¹⁰ ≈ £4,632. Discounting is compounding's mirror, and it converts all of a
    project's or bond's future cash flows into today's terms, where they can be added and compared —
    the <strong>net present value</strong> (NPV) rule says take investments whose discounted inflows
    exceed the discounted cost. Two consequences follow immediately and explain half of financial
    news: distant cash flows shrink violently under discounting (a £1m payment 40 years out is worth
    about £46,000 at 8%), and <em>rising</em> interest rates lower the present value of everything
    already promised — which is why bond prices fall when rates rise, and why growth-company shares
    (whose value sits in far-future profits) are the most rate-sensitive assets.
  </p>

  <h2>Probability: the rate is not a fact</h2>
  <p>
    Everything above assumed r is known. It never is. Next year's return on a savings account is
    nearly certain (this is what "risk-free" means); next year's return on shares is a distribution.
    <strong>Probability theory</strong> is how finance handles this, and the central object is the
    <strong>expected value</strong>: weight each possible outcome by its probability and sum. Worked
    example: an investment returns +50% with probability 0.4 and −20% with probability 0.6. Its
    expected return is 0.4 × 50% + 0.6 × (−20%) = 20% − 12% = +8%. Notice what this says: the most
    <em>likely single outcome</em> (−20%, 60% of the time) is bad, yet the investment can still be
    rational because the occasional +50% pays for the frequent small losses. That structure — skewed
    payoffs, probability-weighted — is the entire logic of venture capital, insurance and lottery
    pricing, in both directions. Expected value also explains why expected returns must beat the
    risk-free rate before risk is worth taking: an 8% expected return on shares exists precisely
    because the distribution includes bad years that a government bond does not.
  </p>

  <h2>Descriptive statistics: mean, variance, and the volatility drag</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Descriptive statistics</strong> then compress the distribution into two numbers. The
    <strong>mean</strong> of historical annual returns says what the asset paid on average; the
    <strong>standard deviation</strong> — the typical distance of yearly returns from that mean — says
    how much it swung. A real-scale illustration: broad equity markets have historically returned
    around 7–10% per year on average (depending on country and period) with a standard deviation near
    15–20%, while short-term government bonds return less with deviations of a few percent. The SD is
    readable through the bell curve: roughly 68% of years fall within one SD of the mean, 95% within
    two — so a 10%-mean, 16%-SD market has about one year in six outside −6% to +26%, and a 95% range
    from about −22% to +42%. That is what "risk" means quantitatively: the width of the annual
    experience, not the sign of the average.
  </p>
  <p>
    Statistics then delivers the lesson's sharpest surprise: <strong>the arithmetic mean overstates
    compounding</strong>. An asset that gains +20% then loses −20% has an arithmetic mean of 0% — but
    £100 becomes £120 and then £96: a <em>loss</em> of 4%, a geometric (compounded) average of about
    −2% per year. Volatility itself destroys compound growth — <strong>volatility drag</strong>, worth
    roughly σ² ÷ 2 per year (a 20% SD costs about 2% of compounded return against the naive mean).
    This is a mathematical fact, not a market quirk, and it is the deep reason diversification is not
    timidity: smoothing the swings genuinely raises long-run compounded wealth for the same average
    return. It is also why funds advertising "average return" rather than compounded (CAGR) figures
    are, technically, telling you something flattering and nearly useless.
  </p>

  <h2>Risk quantification: measuring and taming the swings</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Risk quantification</strong> is the discipline of turning those distributions into
    decisions. The workhorse comparisons: <strong>risk-adjusted return</strong> (the Sharpe ratio —
    excess return over the risk-free rate divided by standard deviation; two portfolios with the same
    return but different SDs are not equivalent, and the ratio says which one paid better per unit of
    bumpy ride), <strong>correlation</strong> (assets that fall at different times, held together,
    cancel part of each other's variance — the only free lunch in the field), and
    <strong>diversification</strong> formalised: combining imperfectly correlated assets reduces
    portfolio SD below any single asset's, without necessarily reducing expected return. Tail measures
    supplement the bell curve, because market returns are famously <em>not</em> normally distributed —
    crashes are more frequent and fatter-tailed than the Gaussian predicts (October 1987's −22.6% day
    in the US was a ~20-sigma event under a normal model): hence Value-at-Risk, stress tests, and the
    humility the 2008 crisis taught about models. Finally, the inflation overlay: a 3% nominal return
    against 3% inflation compounds to zero <em>real</em> growth — "real return" ≈ (1 + nominal) ÷
    (1 + inflation) − 1 — so the rate that matters for long-horizon wealth is always the one net of
    both inflation and fees. A 2% fee on a 6% gross return compounds at 4%, and over 40 years quietly
    removes roughly half the final wealth. Fees, like volatility, are a rate — and every rate
    compounds.
  </p>

  <h2>Compounding in the wild: loans and annuities</h2>
  <p>
    The same formula runs the borrowing side, and a worked amortisation makes it concrete. A
    £200,000 mortgage at 6% annual (0.5% monthly) over 25 years has a monthly payment M solving
    M × [1 − (1.005)⁻³⁰⁰] ÷ 0.005 = 200,000, giving M ≈ £1,289. The revealing part is the
    schedule: in month one, interest is 200,000 × 0.005 = £1,000 — so £1,000 of the £1,289 payment
    is interest and only £289 reduces the debt. Early payments are mostly interest because the
    balance (the base being compounded against you) is largest; by year 20 the split has roughly
    reversed. Total paid over 25 years is about £386,700 — nearly double the loan — which is the
    compound-interest curve pointed the other way, and the reason overpaying early is so powerful:
    an extra £100/month in year one removes years and tens of thousands from the tail, because it
    attacks the balance while the compounding against you is steepest. An <strong>annuity</strong> is
    the same mathematics in reverse — a pot converted to level payments until exhaustion (the
    pension-decumulation problem) — and a <strong>perpetuity</strong> is its limit: a payment that
    never ends is worth simply payment ÷ rate (£1,000/year at 5% = £20,000 today), the cleanest
    single sentence in valuation.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four refinements complete the professional picture. <strong>Continuous compounding</strong>: as
    payments are made ever more frequently, (1 + r/n)ⁿ approaches eʳ — Euler's constant entering money
    the moment compounding is continuous, which is how option-pricing mathematics prefers to write
    growth. <strong>Sequence risk</strong>: two investors with the same <em>average</em> return can end
    differently if the bad years arrive early versus late while they are withdrawing money — the
    retirement-drawing problem that makes "you'll average 8%" cold comfort, and the reason glide paths
    de-risk portfolios as withdrawals begin. <strong>The discount rate is a belief</strong>: valuing
    anything hinges on the r you choose, and r embodies risk appetite, opportunity cost and inflation
    expectations — which is why a central bank raising rates by two points can move asset values by
    trillions without a single cash flow changing: only the discounting changed.
    <strong>Time diversification, stated carefully</strong>: volatility does not shrink with horizon
    in the way intuition hopes (the 30-year SD of compounded <em>outcomes</em> is larger in pounds),
    but the <em>probability</em> of a positive real return does rise with horizon under historical
    distributions — time genuinely is the investor's main edge, which is why starting early dominates
    picking well: a decade of extra compounding at 8% multiplies the final figure by 2.16 before any
    cleverness begins. Internalise the widget's two curves and you hold the subject's whole geometry:
    simple interest is a line, compounding is a curve, discounting is the same curve read backward,
    and risk is the width of the band the curve can honestly be drawn within.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Bogle, The Little Book of Common Sense Investing; Bodie, Kane & Marcus, Investments; Dimson, Marsh & Staunton, Triumph of the Optimists (long-run return data); Ibbotson SBBI yearbooks (US equity risk and volatility).</p>
</TopicPage>
